1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,394
This is a small production-style platform on GKE Autopilot.

2
00:00:04,394 --> 00:00:12,165
A Go microservice called url-health-api registers URLs, probes them every minute and exposes Prometheus metrics.

3
00:00:12,165 --> 00:00:23,475
Every cloud resource is Terraform: the VPC with Cloud NAT, the private Autopilot cluster, Artifact Registry, Workload Identity, Firestore, and the monitoring SLOs.

4
00:00:23,475 --> 00:00:34,716
Delivery is GitOps: GitHub Actions builds and pushes the image with keyless auth, and ArgoCD inside the cluster pulls the manifests and keeps the cluster in sync.

5
00:00:35,891 --> 00:00:41,903
First the running service, through the global external load balancer that the Gateway API created.

6
00:00:41,903 --> 00:00:47,730
I register two URLs, one healthy and one that returns 404, and a bad one that fails validation.

7
00:00:47,730 --> 00:00:54,968
A minute later the checker has probed both: Google's site is up with its latency, the broken one is down with the 404.

8
00:00:54,968 --> 00:00:59,507
The data lives in Firestore, written by the pod through Workload Identity.

9
00:01:01,388 --> 00:01:03,065
Here is the identity chain.

10
00:01:03,065 --> 00:01:15,803
The Kubernetes service account is annotated with the Google service account, the binding grants workload identity user on that account only, and the Google account itself has a single role: datastore user.

11
00:01:15,803 --> 00:01:17,294
There are no keys on it.

12
00:01:17,294 --> 00:01:28,975
The CI identity is similar: a workload identity pool trusts GitHub's OIDC issuer, restricted to this one repository, and maps to a service account that can only write to Artifact Registry.

13
00:01:28,975 --> 00:01:31,772
Nothing in this pipeline stores a credential.

14
00:01:33,135 --> 00:01:34,687
Now the GitOps loop, live.

15
00:01:34,687 --> 00:01:39,103
I change the service, in this case the version endpoint, and push to main.

16
00:01:39,103 --> 00:01:49,248
GitHub Actions runs the tests, builds the image, pushes it to Artifact Registry using the federated token, and then commits the new image tag into the kustomization file.

17
00:01:49,248 --> 00:01:51,039
That commit is the deployment.

18
00:01:52,416 --> 00:01:57,022
This is the workflow run: the test job, then build and push.

19
00:01:57,022 --> 00:02:02,627
The auth step shows the workload identity exchange, no JSON key anywhere.

20
00:02:02,627 --> 00:02:09,000
The last step bumps the tag in k8s slash base and pushes it back to the repository.

21
00:02:15,172 --> 00:02:16,805
ArgoCD watches that path.

22
00:02:16,805 --> 00:02:32,672
Once the tag commit lands, the application shows out of sync for a moment, then the automated sync rolls the deployment: a new ReplicaSet with the new image, surge one, zero unavailable, and the old pods drain after the readiness probe passes.

23
00:02:32,672 --> 00:02:38,548
Self-heal and prune are on, so manual drift is reverted and deleted manifests are removed.

24
00:02:45,222 --> 00:02:53,505
Back on the command line, the deployment now runs the image tagged with the new commit, and the version endpoint returns it through the load balancer.

25
00:02:53,505 --> 00:02:56,598
From git push to a live rollout, no one touched kubectl.

26
00:03:00,431 --> 00:03:04,987
Observability is Google Managed Prometheus scraping the pod's metrics.

27
00:03:04,987 --> 00:03:16,377
On top of that, Cloud Monitoring has a custom service with two SLOs: ninety-nine percent of checks requests under 300 milliseconds, and ninety-nine point five percent non-5xx.

28
00:03:16,377 --> 00:03:27,767
The alert policy is a multi-window burn-rate alert, fourteen-point-four x over one hour or six x over six hours, which pages on sustained budget burn rather than single blips.

29
00:03:30,099 --> 00:03:52,995
The security checklist, verified: private nodes with Cloud NAT, the control plane reachable only from one authorized IP, pods non-root with a read-only root filesystem and all capabilities dropped, a default-deny network policy with explicit allows for the load balancer, the Prometheus collector, DNS and outbound HTTPS, and a distroless image.

30
00:03:54,371 --> 00:04:04,433
Everything shown is in the repository: the Go service with tests, Terraform for the platform, kustomize manifests, the ArgoCD application and the CI workflow.

31
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:12,202
Autopilot bills per pod, so the whole platform costs a few rupees an hour while it runs, and terraform destroy removes it.

32
00:04:12,202 --> 00:04:13,475
Thanks for watching.
